  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Late breakthroughs are only valuable when the pitching holds.",
    "what_this_means": "Roupp gave them five clean innings and the bullpen sealed it. That's not a formula yet, just a result. The eighth-inning rally. Sparked when Jung Hoo Lee worked a walk with the bases empty. Turned what was shaping up as a 1-0 slog into a 6-1 cushion. Jonah Cox and Rafael Devers each had two hits. Nothing spectacular, nothing wasted. This is how the Giants win when they win: careful pitching, enough contact, one inning that matters. The fragility is real. 25 games back in a division that doesn't wait. But a win like this, where the starter didn't labor and the back end stayed clean, is how you stop sliding. Trent Harris threw two scoreless, Carson Seymour and Ryan Walker each handled their own. The difference between slipping and fragile is whether the foundational parts still work. Tonight they did.",
    "what_to_watch": "Kohl Drake is a 5.40 ERA starter, and Arizona's lineup has been held to fewer than four runs in their last two games. If Blade Tidwell can match what Roupp did. Five solid innings, no disasters. The Giants can steal another one tonight before this fragile window closes.",
